A: Unfortunately there is no way to write a "Contract Test" with code contracts to test this particular condition. This is because you don't have the ability to verify the conditions at runtime with code contracts.

In your example, an example of how you could handle this within the method in the ArrayList class is shown below:

```csharp
public void Remove(int x)
{
    // Code Contract: 0 <= IndexOf(x) <= Count
    ...
}
```

Within the specific method `ArrayList.Remove(int x)` where you control the method logic, you can include a code contract. 

However, the pre and post-conditions should be used to describe the external behavior of your class. A contract is a way to document expectations about the behavior of your class, not to test it. Therefore, it's not feasible to use contracts to test these conditions directly from unit tests.

Also, please note that this kind of Pre- and Post-conditions are usually written outside of a method (in class or struct documentation), and not as a part of a unit test validation. Code Contracts are mainly used to guide the developer in anticipating what might happen. It would be more fitting to use them on class or method declaration.

It seems you need to unit test your code on conditions where you can assert that a certain invariant holds. In this case, if you want to test the post-condition of the remove method, your unit test should confirm if `IndexOf(x) <= Count`. 

Here's an example test code:

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void TestArrayListRemove_PostCondition() 
{
  ArrayList arrList = new ArrayList();
  arrList.Add(1);
  arrList.Add(2);
  arrList.Add(3);

  arrList.Remove(2);

  Assert.IsTrue(arrList.IndexOf(2) == -1); // Fails if remove method doesn't work properly
}
```

In this way, you're actually testing an aspect of the ArrayList's behavior that it doesn't normally expose - pre-condition that your collection is well-formed i.e., Contains(x) should be false before the Remove(x) method is invoked. 

If you really want to ensure that all ArrayList's methods work as expected, you should not use ArrayList or any other collection class. You should build your own object that holds a list in an internally controlled manner and makes sure that it obeys the mentioned conditions.

In a nutshell, use Contracts to specify expectations about the behavior of your class, NOT to write an auto-verifying unit test. It's quite different, the second is not really a part of unit testing, but of the broader testing practices in software development.

MSDN: The Code Contracts are there to guide you and to help write loosely coupled, easy-to-maintain code - and they don't really test the code either. They're a way to reduce the amount of testing necessary ... the contract examples are more about how to do a job than about doing a unit test. –  John Wu 

To answer the final part of your question, as stated by the Code Contracts documentation, the best way to use code contracts is to add assert conditions in your code where you want to specify pre-conditions and post-conditions. And never write contract tests, instead, write unit tests to specifically verify the working, and behavior of your code. – John Wu
Please note that, although Code Contracts provides a powerful tool, it's a very old technology. So use it wisely. – John Wu

Microsoft Code Contracts are a powerful tool designed to help write more robust, correct, and maintainable code. However, Code Contracts are not designed to be used as traditional unit testing framework. The reason is that Code Contracts does not actually perform runtime checks. Instead, it compiles your method into a callable function and embeds information about preconditions, postconditions and other method annotations.

In the end, to effectively use code contracts it is important to remember the distinction between what code contracts are for and what testing tools are for. 

The job of testing and contract checking is to ensure that your code behaves as expected in every situation, not just one particular one. 

If this seems to involve a large amount of work, it is, but the time to write those contracts is not that long, and can usually be factored into the overall development time--------------------	    AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(Old_arrListIndexOfX >= -1);
	    AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(New_arrListIndexOfX >= -1);

            if (Old_arrListContainsX)
            {
                Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListCount - 1, New_arrListCount);
                Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListIndexOfX < New_arrListIndexOfX || Old_arrListIndexOfX == -1, Old_arrListIndexOfX == New_arrListIndexOfX, "IndexOf");
                if (Old_arrListIndexOfX != -1)
                {
                    Assert.AreEqual(-1, New_arrListIndexOfX, "IndexOf");
                    Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListIndexOfX, New_arrListLastIndexOfX, "LastIndexOf");
                    Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListIndexOfX, arrList.IndexOf(Old_x), "ArrayList.IndexOf");
                }
                Assert.AreNotEqual(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX, New_arrListLastIndexOfX, "LastIndexOf");
                Assert.AreNotEqual(Old_arrListIndexOfX != -1, arrList.Contains(Old_x), "ArrayList.Contains");
            }
            else
            {
                if (Old_arrListIndexOfX != -1)
                {
                    Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListIndexOfX, arrList.IndexOf(Old_x), "ArrayList.IndexOf");
                }
                Assert.AreEqual(New_arrListCount, Old_arrListCount);
            }
            if (New_arrListContainsX)
                Assert.Fail();
        }
    }
}

```csharp
// Fields defined in a test class using PexAssembly attribute should be ignored by Pex
public class ArrayListContractTest
{
  // This ArrayList field is ignored by Pex as it does not provide any functional benefits
  // The test class does not implement any functionality that uses the ArrayList field
  [PexAssembly]
  ArrayList arrList = new ArrayList();
}
```

In this testing scenario, a precondition is assumed, `AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);` In order to carry out the `arrList.Remove(x);` command. 

In addition, before the `Remove()` test method is run, I've put in an assumption that'll return a boolean test (`AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(Old_arrListIndexOfX >= -1);`). This validates if the old index of `x` is greater or equal to `-1`; likewise, it checks that the new index of 'x' is also greater or equal to `-1`.

The Pex test observer is used to view important states of the array.

Following this, I've done assertions on the expected changes such as the count of the array, the index of 'x', the last index of 'x', and the array contains 'x'.

If `x` is present in the array before the removal, the count after the invocation of `Remove(x)` method should decrease by 1 and the index of 'x' in the new array should be the same as the old index or less (-1 in case of an only one 'x' in the array), the index of 'x' in the new array should not be the same as the old index, the last index of 'x' should be the same as the old index or less (-1 in case of only one 'x' in the array), `Contains(x)` should return `false` in the new array; if 'x' isn't in the array before removal, the count after the removal should be the same as in the old array, and the index of 'x' in the new array should be -1 (not present).

At the end, if `x` was in the array before the removal, it confirms that removal isn't successful, then the test method fails. 

This ensures that `Remove(x)` method doesn't return an exception that potentially leaves the array in an undesirable state. The verification confirms that the method doesn't affect other elements.

This test method covers negative scenarios too and checks the conditions for removed element, 'x'. 

This makes it an effective `Remove` test that's rigorous and verifies the behavior of `ArrayList`. For larger programs, such a contract-based testing approach would be highly beneficial.
