I need to adapt the above code to work with a List instead of an ArrayList. I understand it can be achieved by using List class. But, I'm confused because the code has lines that involve array index, such as: arrList.IndexOf(x), even though the data structure really isn't an array but a list. How do those lines of code know to get the correct behavior just by using List instead of ArrayList?
To clarify, why does it work the same with ArrayList?
The original ArrayListTest.cs code is copied from http://research.microsoft.com/en-us/um/cambridge/groups/ppl/code/contracts/ArrayList/2014/07/ArrayListTest_CodeCopied.zip
Why doesn't List have an IndexOf method?

A: 
Why does it work the same with ArrayList?

The ArrayList class in .NET is a wrapper around a dynamically sized array. Under the hood, it holds an array of objects and when an item is added or removed, if the array needs to be resized, it creates a new larger or smaller array, copies all the items over and then disposes of the old array. This allows it to handle lists of any size very efficiently.

ArrayList and List differ in how they handle addition and deletion of items. When you add an item to a List, it is simply added to the end of the list. When you remove an item, it is simply overwritten. Both data structures are implemented with arrays under the hood, but they handle array resizing and item movement differently.

ArrayList's IndexOf method works the same because it uses an internal array index to keep track of where items are stored. When an item is added, it is placed at the next open slot in the array, and when it is removed, that slot is marked as free to be reused. So, even though it looks like the items are not stored in the same order as in a standard List, the internal array allows it to keep track of the items efficiently.


Why doesn't List have an IndexOf method?

The .NET framework's List<T> class has no direct equivalent to the ArrayList's IndexOf method because it does not behave identically.

Just as an ArrayList is optimized for speed at the cost of having to deal with array resizing, a List<T> in .NET is optimized for speed and ease of use at the cost of having to deal with resizing.

The way a List handles items is different from an ArrayList. When you add an item, it is simply placed in the next available slot in the underlying array. When you remove an item, it is simply overwritten in place. Both data structures efficiently store and retrieve items, but they do it in very different ways.

The IndexOf method in List works by iterating over all items in the list until it finds the one you're looking for. This is much slower for large lists because it has to check every item. This is why it is slower than getting the item directly by index, which just gets it from the underlying array.

By not providing an IndexOf method, List<T> aims to strike a balance between the speed of direct array access and the clarity and speed of iterative searching like List does with its own implementation.

